      "updated": 1780817649896,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Message Ordering Consistency\n- The Problem: Concurrent messages from different devices can arrive out\nof order, confusing chat flow.\n- Option 1 (Client Timestamps): Unreliable due to clock drift and potential\nmanipulation.\n- Option 2 (DB Sequence Counter): Chat server assigns a strict sequence\nnumber per chat. Good for medium scale, but atomic increments become a\nbottleneck at high scale.\n- Option 3 (Kafka-Based Ingestion - Recommended): Route messages\nthrough Kafka, partitioning by chat_id. Kafka enforces strict serial\nordering per partition before writing to the DB.\n- Hot Shard Alert: \"Celebrity\" or \"All-Hands\" chats can overwhelm a single\nKafka partition. Mitigate with dedicated high-throughput topics or hybrid\nfan-out models.\n\n2) Scaling WebSocket Infrastructure\n- Bottleneck 1: Session Lookup & Fan-out: Querying a DB for active\nsessions on every message is too slow.\n    > Solution: Shift to Pub/Sub (Redis/Kafka). WS servers subscribe only\nto chat_channel:<chat_id> for their currently connected users.\n- Bottleneck 2: User Churn (Moving between WS servers): Rapid\ndisconnects/reconnects leave stale subscriptions and cause duplicate fan-\nouts.\n    > Solution (Leased Ownership): WS servers set a Redis TTL lease (e.g.,\nuser:A = ws-1 EX 10). Only the server holding the valid lease subscribes to\nthat user's chat channels. If the user drops, the lease expires, stopping\nwasted fan-out.\n- Bottleneck 3: DB Storage Pressure: Massive write load for group chat\nfan-outs and read load for reconnections.\n    > Solution: Horizontally partition messages (by chat_id) and inbox (by\nuser_id). Use Redis/Memcached to cache hot metadata (chat members,\nactive sessions, recent messages) to protect the DB.\n\n3) Multi-Device Management\n- The Problem: Users have multiple active devices (phone, laptop). They\nneed real-time delivery on all active screens, reliable replay on disconnected\nones, and no duplicate messages.\n- The Flow:\n    > Devices register individually in device_sessions (e.g., User:A:Phone ->\nws-1, User:A:Laptop -> ws-2).\n    > WS servers fan-out messages to all active sessions for that user.\n    > If a specific device is offline, the message goes to the inbox keyed to\nthat exact device_id.\n- Deduplication (The Silent Killer): A user might get a message via WS on\ntheir phone, but via Inbox Replay on their laptop.\n    > Solution: Every message has a unique message_id. Clients maintain a\nlocal cache of recent IDs. If a client receives a message it already has, it\nsilently drops it. Never rely purely on backend deduplication.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Message Ordering Consistency\n- The Problem: Concurrent messages from different devices can arrive out of order, confusing chat flow.\n- Option 1 (Client Timestamps): Unreliable due to clock drift and potential manipulation.\n- Option 2 (DB Sequence Counter): Chat server assigns a strict sequence number per chat. Good for medium scale, but atomic increments become a bottleneck at high scale.\n- Option 3 (Kafka-Based Ingestion - Recommended): Route messages through Kafka, partitioning by chat_id. Kafka enforces strict serial ordering per partition before writing to the DB.\n- Hot Shard Alert: \"Celebrity\" or \"All-Hands\" chats can overwhelm a single Kafka partition. Mitigate with dedicated high-throughput topics or hybrid fan-out models.\n\n2) Scaling WebSocket Infrastructure\n- Bottleneck 1: Session Lookup & Fan-out: Querying a DB for active sessions on every message is too slow.\n    > Solution: Shift to Pub/Sub (Redis/Kafka). WS servers subscribe only to chat_channel:<chat_id> for their currently connected users.\n- Bottleneck 2: User Churn (Moving between WS servers): Rapid disconnects/reconnects leave stale subscriptions and cause duplicate fan-outs.\n    > Solution (Leased Ownership): WS servers set a Redis TTL lease (e.g., user:A = ws-1 EX 10). Only the server holding the valid lease subscribes to that user's chat channels. If the user drops, the lease expires, stopping wasted fan-out.\n- Bottleneck 3: DB Storage Pressure: Massive write load for group chat fan-outs and read load for reconnections.\n    > Solution: Horizontally partition messages (by chat_id) and inbox (by user_id). Use Redis/Memcached to cache hot metadata (chat members, active sessions, recent messages) to protect the DB.\n\n3) Multi-Device Management\n- The Problem: Users have multiple active devices (phone, laptop). They need real-time delivery on all active screens, reliable replay on disconnected ones, and no duplicate messages.\n- The Flow:\n    > Devices register individually in device_sessions (e.g., User:A:Phone -> ws-1, User:A:Laptop -> ws-2).\n    > WS servers fan-out messages to all active sessions for that user.\n    > If a specific device is offline, the message goes to the inbox keyed to that exact device_id.\n- Deduplication (The Silent Killer): A user might get a message via WS on their phone, but via Inbox Replay on their laptop.\n    > Solution: Every message has a unique message_id. Clients maintain a local cache of recent IDs. If a client receives a message it already has, it silently drops it. Never rely purely on backend deduplication.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
