    {
      "id": "ZkBBWKb1vT2HifWUbXKkq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1575.154490780718,
      "y": 80.89308784110017,
      "width": 732.9198328673299,
      "height": 1700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bI9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1874489904,
      "version": 500,
      "versionNonce": 1661178928,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1780817649896,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Message Deletion & Storage\n- Never delete a message from the database just because it was delivered\nto the user's phone.\n- Keep messages saved for chat history and syncing across devices.\n- Only delete a message if the sender explicitly deletes it, or if there is an\nautomatic rule (like auto-deleting after 30 days).\n\n2) Threaded Conversations\n- What are threads? Threads let users reply directly to a specific\nmessage, creating a focused, nested mini-chat inside the main conversation\n(like Slack).\n- How to build it: Instead of creating a complex new system for threads,\njust add an optional parent_message_id to standard messages.\n- This simple change makes it easy to load thread replies and send\nnotifications only to the people chatting in that specific thread.\n\n3) Syncing Multiple Devices\n- Users are often logged into their phone and laptop at the same time.\n- The system must ensure \"read\" statuses sync perfectly across all\nscreens and stop duplicate push notifications from buzzing every device in\nthe room.\n\n4) \"Client\" vs. \"DeviceSession\"\n- A Client is just the software platform (e.g., the iOS app or a Web\nbrowser).\n- A DeviceSession is the actual, active connection between that device\nand the server.\n- Why track sessions? It tells the server exactly how to route messages\n(e.g., via WebSocket to an open laptop, or via Push Notification to a\nlocked phone), accurately tracks granular online/offline presence, and \n- enforces the cross-device consistency mentioned above.\n\n5) Smart Read Receipts\n- Don't mark a message as \"read\" just because it reached the device.\n- The app should only send a read_receipt event to the server when the\nmessage actually appears on the user's screen.\n- The server then updates the database and updates the sender's screen\nwith \"Read\" or double-ticks.\n\n6) Direct Media Downloads: The backend only stores a media_url. Clients\ndownload heavy files directly from object storage (like S3) to prevent\nbackend bottlenecking and save bandwidth.\n\n7) Online vs. Offline Presence: \"Online\" strictly means an active WebSocket\nconnection (instant delivery). \"Offline\" means no active connection\n(triggers push notifications). This state is tracked via a DeviceSession\ntable.\n\n8) Push ≠ Delivery: Push notifications just wake the device using\nmetadata. True delivery only occurs when the user opens the app,\nreconnects to WebSockets, and pulls the actual message payload from the\nbackend.\n\n9) 30-Day Offline Rule: To save space, undelivered queue records expire\nafter 30 days. The original message stays safe in the main database, but\nreturning users must manually fetch it via chat history APIs instead of\nauto-receiving it.\n\n10) Clean Message Deletion: No ugly \"message deleted\" placeholders. The\nbackend soft-deletes the record (is_deleted = true), but the frontend\ninstantly performs a hard visual wipe for online users and hides it from\noffline users' history entirely.\n\n11) Database Trade-offs: Relational databases (MySQL + Vitess) offer\nstrong consistency and easy joins but are harder to scale. NoSQL\n(DynamoDB) scales massively with low latency but sacrifices flexible\nqueries and introduces eventual consistency. Always debate the trade-offs\nrather than just naming a tech stack.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Message Deletion & Storage\n- Never delete a message from the database just because it was delivered to the user's phone.\n- Keep messages saved for chat history and syncing across devices.\n- Only delete a message if the sender explicitly deletes it, or if there is an automatic rule (like auto-deleting after 30 days).\n\n2) Threaded Conversations\n- What are threads? Threads let users reply directly to a specific message, creating a focused, nested mini-chat inside the main conversation (like Slack).\n- How to build it: Instead of creating a complex new system for threads, just add an optional parent_message_id to standard messages.\n- This simple change makes it easy to load thread replies and send notifications only to the people chatting in that specific thread.\n\n3) Syncing Multiple Devices\n- Users are often logged into their phone and laptop at the same time.\n- The system must ensure \"read\" statuses sync perfectly across all screens and stop duplicate push notifications from buzzing every device in the room.\n\n4) \"Client\" vs. \"DeviceSession\"\n- A Client is just the software platform (e.g., the iOS app or a Web browser).\n- A DeviceSession is the actual, active connection between that device and the server.\n- Why track sessions? It tells the server exactly how to route messages (e.g., via WebSocket to an open laptop, or via Push Notification to a locked phone), accurately tracks granular online/offline presence, and \n- enforces the cross-device consistency mentioned above.\n\n5) Smart Read Receipts\n- Don't mark a message as \"read\" just because it reached the device.\n- The app should only send a read_receipt event to the server when the message actually appears on the user's screen.\n- The server then updates the database and updates the sender's screen with \"Read\" or double-ticks.\n\n6) Direct Media Downloads: The backend only stores a media_url. Clients download heavy files directly from object storage (like S3) to prevent backend bottlenecking and save bandwidth.\n\n7) Online vs. Offline Presence: \"Online\" strictly means an active WebSocket connection (instant delivery). \"Offline\" means no active connection (triggers push notifications). This state is tracked via a DeviceSession table.\n\n8) Push ≠ Delivery: Push notifications just wake the device using metadata. True delivery only occurs when the user opens the app, reconnects to WebSockets, and pulls the actual message payload from the backend.\n\n9) 30-Day Offline Rule: To save space, undelivered queue records expire after 30 days. The original message stays safe in the main database, but returning users must manually fetch it via chat history APIs instead of auto-receiving it.\n\n10) Clean Message Deletion: No ugly \"message deleted\" placeholders. The backend soft-deletes the record (is_deleted = true), but the frontend instantly performs a hard visual wipe for online users and hides it from offline users' history entirely.\n\n11) Database Trade-offs: Relational databases (MySQL + Vitess) offer strong consistency and easy joins but are harder to scale. NoSQL (DynamoDB) scales massively with low latency but sacrifices flexible queries and introduces eventual consistency. Always debate the trade-offs rather than just naming a tech stack.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
